  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 216 PM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: Isolated to scattered showers will continue \nbehind the front this afternoon into the evening. Once the sun \nsets, shower chances decrease with drier air working into the \nregion. Through the night into Tuesday, northwesterly breezes \nwill continue and slowly decrease. As such, the Lake Wind \nAdvisory will continue into the evening hours. Gusts 20-30 mph \ncan be expected in the Champlain Valley, especially for \nlocations adjacent to Lake Champlain, into early Tuesday before \nslowly decreasing. Dry air aloft is evident in soundings and \nwill lower to the surface as temperatures warm into the 50s \nresulting in lower than usual RH for these temperatures. The \ngradient weakens further Tuesday night with weak ridging \ncresting. This will support areas of frost, especially outside \nthe Champlain Valley, as temperatures drop into the low/mid 30s \nfor most valleys, except 20s to low 30s in the Adirondacks and \nnortheastern Vermont. \n\nThe next wave approaches Wednesday with winds increasing again \nand turning out of the south. The primary wave will be displaced\ntowards the James Bay, so best chances of showers will remain \nnearer to the Canadian border. Models favor stalling the parent \nlow resulting in continued chances into Thursday as subsequent \nwaves pinwheel around the low. Current guidance points to \ntemperatures warming into the mid 60s Thursday supporting some \nisolated thunderstorm chances Thursday afternoon. Still, no \nlarge amounts of rain are expected with totals generally staying\nless then 0.25\". \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: Heading towards the end of the week, seasonably \ncool conditions and periodic showers are expected. Daytime high \ntemperatures will climb into the 50s to low 60s, with overnight \nlows in the 30s to mid 40s. An upper low to our north will allow\nfor periods of showers throughout this week, especially across \nthe higher terrain and locations further north towards the \nborder. Although there will be several chances for showers, \ndrier conditions look to return towards the weekend as ridging \nbuilds back into the region, although trends will need to be \nmonitored as we get closer.",
